# Output
## Test Ownership Policy for Brownfield Fixes

### Scope
This policy applies to every brownfield fix in this project: MOCK3-1 (riders aged exactly 65 are charged the adult fare) in mocka, and MOCK3-2 (the departure board omits the final stop) in mockb. Both codelines share the same stack — vitest and typescript — so the policy is uniform across them.

### Who owns the test
The implementer role — typescript-vitest-engineer — owns the test for every brownfield fix. "Owns" means the implementer writes the test, runs it under vitest, and is accountable for it passing before the fix is considered complete. There is no second implementer to hand the test to; this role spans both codelines, so ownership never transfers.

### Who does not own the test
The investigator roles — mocka-fares-investigator and mockb-schedule-investigator — characterise the defect and produce evidence: the expected behaviour, the root cause, and the surrounding logic that must be understood before the implementer touches the code. They do not write, modify, or own tests. Their output informs the implementer's test but does not substitute for one.

### Writer and reviewer agreement
This single policy is rendered into both the writer and the reviewer so that neither can hold a belief the other contradicts:

- The writer must not assume an investigator will provide the test, nor that a fix needs no test because the investigator already characterised the defect.
- The reviewer must not reject a fix on the grounds that an investigator should have written the test, nor accept a fix that lacks a test on the grounds that the investigator's characterisation is sufficient.
- Both must agree on one rule: no brownfield fix is complete without a vitest test, written by typescript-vitest-engineer, that demonstrates the fix and would fail without it.
